@dan-f, I had a come-to-Jesus moment with @catong about the naming of cohorts and content groups while reviewing the doc. We're making the following change across the platform: 

(old) Cohort group(s) = (new) Cohort(s)
(old) Cohorted content group(s) = (new) Content group(s)

@cahrens mentioned that we should make this change in this PR to the areas you're touching, so that you can not only sync up the naming of the UI text strings but also your class names. 

Can you please make this change? Let me know if you have questions.

@andy-armstrong - when you say "both sections should be alphabetical," are you suggesting that we order both content groups and experiment configurations alphabetically? Or that we order content groups and groups _belonging to_ experiment configurations alphabetically?

One issue is whether or not we sort the collection the moment a model is added (can control this by passing {sort: true/false} as an option to collection.add(). I opted not to do this, since the UX of adding a group to the bottom of the list and seeing it inserted in the middle can be confusing, but there are quirks in either case.

@explorerleslie any thoughts on the ordering of content groups/experiment configurations/groups within configurations?

@dan-f @explorerleslie Having randomly ordered UIs is one of my pet peeves. Without delete or reorder, it means that if the user ends up with a group that they don't want they will be stuck with it sitting in the middle of their list. Having alphabetical ordering allows the user to find an item easily when they have a few, and it also makes it possible to use a naming convention to move items to the end.

BTW, maybe the problem is that you were sorting the model which is unnecessary (and incorrect as it it sending an updated order back to the server). I was thinking that you'd just sort the collection (non-destructively) when you iterate it to produce the child views. Was the UX problem that the new item wasn't necessarily added at the end?

Anyway, I understand that given the tight deadline it isn't something we should do now. However, we should always consider ordering in the future as having a seemingly random order is never the right solution. Showing items in the order they were created is fine if only one author is working on the project, but as we do re-runs and other authors come and go, then that order ends up appearing to be random.

@muhammad-ammar I'm starting my review by looking at the sandbox. 

My main impression is that I really don't like the navigation. I was expecting to see the name of the unit where I was going to move it to, but all I saw was a list of chapters. My first impression was that clicking on a chapter would move the component there. I decided to click on one and then saw that it was more of a drill-down (I guess that's what the => means). I then found it difficult to find the unit I wanted because I had to drill in and out to multiple levels, and then drill down again remembering which path I'd already been down.

IMO reusing the course outline would be a much better UX because it is easier to expand and collapse in place to find the unit you're looking for. It also is visually consistent with the rest of Studio, and authors are used to working with the tree.

There was one more URL that I forgot to mention: The import status. It's listed in the description now.

@cpennington 
1. Yep, course_key was what I meant; updated the description.
2. The **/api/v1/courses/{_course_key_}** end point is intended to be a RESTful endpoint for entire courses. Right now, it only offers _application/x-tgz_ responses to GET, and meets the request with a 406 if it demands otherwise. Given the treatment of courses like self-contained objects here, it seems unexpected to render other content types in response to a GET here, unless of course there are other response types that can practically and accurately represent an entire full course object.

